{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoWr6BJndDts4ngjQiLL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`python3 -m pytest -q` gives 57 passed, but `TokenBucket.wait_time` has a performance bug that tests with larger clock values would expose. The author's tests don't use large clock values.\n\n**Problem: `ledgerkit/limiter.py:55-66`, `wait_time` can take a very long time when the clock value is large**\n- The correction loop raises `seconds` one `math.nextafter(seconds, inf)` step at a time. Each step does `Fraction` arithmetic.\n- `last_time + seconds` is quantized to the spacing of floats near `last_time`. When `last_time` is large and `seconds` is small, that spacing is far coarser than the spacing of `seconds`.\n- Reaching the next distinct clock value then takes about ulp(`last_time`) / ulp(`seconds`) iterations.\n- Failing scenario: rate=3, capacity=5, fake clock at 1000091.6666666677, tokens=2**-32, `wait_time(1.5)`. That needs roughly 1e6 iterations, and the process was still running after 10s.\n- This is a realistic case: `time.monotonic()` is often around 1e5 to 1e7 seconds, and larger on long-running hosts.\n- I ran a randomized stress script with rate 3 or 1e5 and a clock starting at 1e6. It never finished. Rates 0.1 and 1e-3 finished.\n\n**Fix**\n1. In `wait_time`, search over candidate clock times instead of candidate durations.\n   - Start with `target = last_time + seconds`.\n   - Test `delta = target - last_time` with `self._tokens + Fraction(delta) * self._rate >= amount`.\n   - If that fails, set `target = math.nextafter(target, math.inf)`. Each step moves to the next distinct clock value, so only a few iterations are needed.\n   - Return `target - last_time` once the test passes.\n2. Keep the current guards for non-finite values and for `target == last_time`.\n3. Add a test with clock start values like 1e6 and 1e9, rates 3, 7 and 0.1, partial balances, and fractional `n`, over a few thousand cycles. Each `wait_time` call should finish quickly, and the following `try_acquire(n)` should succeed.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":445,"cache_read_input_tokens":37167,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":445},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d1dc30d3-68eb-4900-9f9d-7b33b23f8dc1","uuid":"c609d154-c728-4ee7-86cb-97893df4db87","timestamp":"2026-11-07T11:48:46.610Z","request_id":"req_011CgoWr61tKN91MXfsh3mTF"}
